# MCP Server Rules

The MCP (Model Context Protocol) server exposes the design system to AI editors. It lives in `src/mcp/` and is served via `app/mcp/route.ts`.

## Available tools

The MCP server exposes exactly these nine tools — no more, no less:

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `start_project` | Returns the brief to gather, then the resolved build plan. Withholds the plan until the required answers arrive |
| `check_version` | Returns the current release, the changelog, and a verdict on a project's installed version |
| `scaffold_app` | Returns the ordered commands to create a new app on the user's own disk, brand wired |
| `list_components` | Lists all components grouped by category, led by the published patterns |
| `get_component` | Returns full spec for a named component, plus its related components and the patterns it appears in |
| `get_tokens` | Returns design tokens for a category |
| `get_pattern` | Returns full page layout code |
| `search_docs` | Searches across components, patterns, guidelines, changelog |
| `get_installation` | Returns framework-specific setup steps (nextjs, react) |

If you need to add a tool, get approval first — every tool added is a new prompt-time API surface.

## The version gate

`src/mcp/version-gate.ts` decides whether a response is allowed to carry code. The server serves whatever the deployed site was built from; the project on the other end is on whatever it installed, and Atoms installs from a git ref so those routinely differ. When they do, an agent pastes an API the project does not have, the import fails, and the agent repairs its own mistake by inventing a plausible prop.

One rule, and it is the whole design:

> **The gate withholds pasteable code. It never withholds knowledge.**

Props, taglines, do/don't lists, token values and related-component pointers go out regardless — reading them cannot break a build, and an agent refused everything stops asking and starts guessing. Code blocks are what get held, because a code block is an instruction to paste.

- `get_component` and `get_pattern` take an optional `installedVersion` and withhold their code until it resolves. Optional, not required: a required argument turns every existing call into a validation error rather than a version warning.
- Four of the seven statuses block, and only one is "too old". The other three are forms of not knowing, which is the common case for a git install.
- A new code-serving tool must take `installedVersionSchema` and call `gateVersion`. Both are exported from `version-gate.ts` so no tool writes its own copy of the wording.

## Releasing

A release bumps three things in one commit, and `scripts/generate.ts` fails the build if they disagree:

1. `package.json` `version`
2. `ATOMS_VERSION` in `src/release/version.ts`
3. A new entry at the top of `src/release/changelog.ts`

Then tag it — `git tag v<version> && git push --tags` — or a `#semver:` install cannot resolve it. Set `scope` on any change that moves a component's API: that is what lets `check_version` tell a caller which of the things they are about to use changed, without a `since` field on every component.

Bump `minimumSupported` in `src/release/index.ts` only when a release actually removes or renames something the served code uses. It is the oldest version that code still runs on, not the oldest version anyone should be on.

## Server instructions

`createAtomsMcpServer` passes `instructions` to the `McpServer` constructor. That string is the only guidance a client reads *before* deciding which tool to call, which makes it the only place the order of operations can live — a tool description is read once the tool is already being considered, and by then an agent that decided to write a dashboard from scratch has already stopped looking for one.

Keep it short and ordered. Anything that only matters once a specific tool is in play belongs in that tool's `description` instead.

Bump the server version when the tool surface changes; a client may report it.

## Tool implementation pattern

Each tool lives in `src/mcp/tools/<tool-name>.ts`:

```tsx
// src/mcp/tools/get-component.ts
import { z } from 'zod';
import componentsData from '@/data/components.json';

export const getComponentTool = {
  name: 'get_component',
  description: 'Get full details for a specific component: props with TypeScript types, defaults, code examples, do\'s and don\'ts, and related components. Always call this before using a component to get the correct API.',
  schema: {
    name: z.string().describe('Component name, e.g. "Button", "TextField". Case-sensitive.'),
  },
  handler: async ({ name }: { name: string }) => {
    const comp = (componentsData as any)[name];
    if (!comp) {
      const available = Object.keys(componentsData).join(', ');
      return {
        content: [{ type: 'text', text: `Component "${name}" not found. Available: ${available}` }],
      };
    }
    return {
      content: [{ type: 'text', text: formatComponentResponse(comp) }],
    };
  },
};
```

## Tool description rules

The `description` field is what the AI editor reads to decide when to call the tool. Make them:

1. **Action-oriented** — what does it return, when should AI use it
2. **Include "always" language** for tools that should be called proactively
3. **Mention the format** — "Returns markdown table of props..." helps the AI know what to expect

```tsx
// ✅ Good
description: 'Get full component spec including props, types, examples, and do\'s/don\'ts. Always call before using a component to get the correct API.'

// ❌ Bad
description: 'Returns component info'
```

## Data loading

The MCP server reads from `data/*.json` files generated by `scripts/generate.ts`:

```tsx
// src/mcp/data-loader.ts
import componentsData from '@/data/components.json';
import tokensData from '@/data/tokens.json';
import patternsData from '@/data/patterns.json';

export { componentsData, tokensData, patternsData };
```

**Never read from `src/components/` directly in the MCP server.** Always go through `data/`. The generator script is the single bridge between source code and MCP data.

## Generator script

The generator (`scripts/generate.ts`) is the only file that reads from `src/components/` to produce `data/`:

```tsx
// scripts/generate.ts
import * as fs from 'fs';
import * as path from 'path';
import { glob } from 'glob';

async function generate() {
  // Find all component examples files
  const exampleFiles = await glob('src/components/**/*.examples.tsx');

  // Read each, extract data
  const components = {};
  for (const file of exampleFiles) {
    const componentName = path.basename(file, '.examples.tsx');
    const data = await import(path.resolve(file));
    components[componentName] = data.data;
  }

  // Write to data/
  fs.writeFileSync('data/components.json', JSON.stringify(components, null, 2));

  // Same for tokens and patterns
  // ...

  console.log('✅ Generated data files');
}

generate();
```

Always run `npm run generate` after:
- Adding a new component
- Changing a component's API
- Updating tokens
- Adding or modifying patterns

## Response format

MCP tool responses are markdown text. Structure them for AI consumption:

```tsx
function formatComponentResponse(comp: ComponentData) {
  return `
# ${comp.name}

${comp.tagline}

**Import:** \`import { ${comp.name} } from '@neoflo/atoms';\`

## Props

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
${comp.props.map(p => `| \`${p.name}\` | \`${p.type}\` | \`${p.default}\` | ${p.description} |`).join('\n')}

## Examples

${comp.examples.map(e => `### ${e.title}\n\`\`\`tsx\n${e.code}\n\`\`\``).join('\n\n')}

## ✅ Do
${comp.dos.map(d => `- ${d}`).join('\n')}

## ❌ Don't
${comp.donts.map(d => `- ${d}`).join('\n')}
  `.trim();
}
```

## HTTP transport in Next.js

The MCP server uses HTTP transport via a Next.js Route Handler:

```tsx
// app/mcp/route.ts
import { handleMcpRequest } from '@/src/mcp/server';
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';

export async function POST(req: NextRequest) {
  const auth = req.headers.get('authorization');
  if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production' && auth !== `Bearer ${process.env.MCP_TOKEN}`) {
    return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Unauthorized' }, { status: 401 });
  }
  const body = await req.json();
  return NextResponse.json(await handleMcpRequest(body));
}
```

## Authentication

- **Development:** No auth required (localhost only)
- **Production:** Bearer token required, set via `MCP_TOKEN` env var
- **Token rotation:** Coordinate with platform team if rotating — engineers must update `.cursor/mcp.json`

## Versioning

The MCP server reports its version in the `serverInfo` response. Bump it when:
- **Patch:** Bug fixes, data updates (1.0.0 → 1.0.1)
- **Minor:** New tools added, non-breaking changes (1.0.0 → 1.1.0)
- **Major:** Tool signature changes, removed tools (1.0.0 → 2.0.0)

Consumers can pin to a major version if needed.
